<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volta River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Volta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Volta is a river system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> that flows south through present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. Around 1748, at the height of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> power, its tributary network stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> in the east to the Volta corridor in the west. This area encompassed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volta-river.jpg" alt="Volta River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Siyajkak at Japanese Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VoltaRiverWithAdombeBridge183-1-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
